        <div id="drive-wheels" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Front, Rear or All-Wheel Drive: Which Self-Propelled Mower Is Best?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rear-wheel drive is the right choice for sloped yards and for anyone who bags clippings, while front-wheel drive suits flat lots with plenty of turns. Every self-propelled mower sends engine or motor power to one set of wheels through a belt or a small transmission, and which set gets it shapes how the mower behaves more than any other line on the spec sheet. Settle the drive layout first, then compare engines, batteries and decks only within that group.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When is front-wheel drive enough?</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Front-wheel drive</strong> is the most common layout and the cheapest. With the powered wheels at the front, you can press down on the handle, lift them clear of the grass and spin the mower on its rear wheels, which makes it easy to steer around trees, beds and play sets. Its weak point is weight transfer. Push uphill, or let the rear bag fill, and weight comes off the front wheels until they begin to slip. On a flat lot you will never notice. On a sloped front yard you will notice every single week.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why does rear-wheel drive grip better on hills?</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Rear-wheel drive</strong> puts the power under the weight. As you climb and as the bag gets heavier, the rear wheels press harder into the turf and gain traction instead of losing it. That is why rear drive is the standard advice for hills and for regular baggers. The price is a little more effort in turns, because you cannot lift the drive wheels to pivot. Most owners get used to it within a couple of mows by turning on the rear wheels and backing up a touch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is all-wheel drive worth the extra money?</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>All-wheel drive</strong> is less common and costs more. It earns its keep on very uneven, steep or wet ground where even a rear-drive mower sometimes spins a wheel. A typical suburban yard does not need it. If you mow a genuine hillside or soggy low ground, the premium can be justified; otherwise, put the money toward a better rear-drive mower.
          </p>

