        <div id="speed-control" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Single Speed, Variable Speed or Personal Pace: Which Is Easiest to Use?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pace-sensing handle is the easiest to live with, a variable-speed dial is the sensible middle ground, and a single fixed speed works well only if it happens to match your stride. After the drive wheels, the way you set the speed decides more than anything else whether mowing feels easy. Budget mowers often run at one fixed speed, mid-range models add a dial or lever, and the more refined systems, such as Toro's Personal Pace and similar pressure-sensing bars, read how hard you push and adjust on their own.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is a single-speed drive a deal-breaker?</h3>

          <p>
            Not necessarily. One fixed speed is limiting, but if you walk at a steady pace across an open lot, that speed simply becomes your mowing pace. Trouble starts when the speed and the walker do not match: taller owners often find budget drives too slow, while shorter or older owners often feel the mower pulling ahead of them. Try the drive in the store before you buy, or look for owner reviews that talk about speed specifically.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What do dials and pace-sensing handles add?</h3>

          <p>
            A variable-speed dial lets you choose a pace and hold it, which helps in thick grass, where slowing down gives the blade time to cut and lets the bag fill evenly. A pace-sensing handle goes a step further: you just walk and the mower follows. Around obstacles that is a real advantage, since the mower slows when you slow without you moving a hand. It does add one more mechanism to maintain, though owners rarely report problems with it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do battery mowers control speed?</h3>

          <p>
            Battery mowers set speed electronically, which usually feels smooth and lets some models offer a wider speed range than belt-driven gas drives. Whatever the system, a slower setting is the right choice in tall growth. Hurrying a self-propelled mower through overgrown grass is the most common cause of a ragged cut, a clogged chute and a flat battery.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gas-or-battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Should You Buy a Gas or Battery Self-Propelled Mower?
          </h2>

          <p>
            On a mostly flat lawn under about a third of an acre, a battery mower is usually the better buy; gas still makes sense on larger, steeper or often-overgrown lawns. Battery self-propelled mowers have improved enough to be the stronger choice for many suburban lots, and the gap that remains is about runtime under heavy load rather than cut quality.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where does a battery mower win?</h3>

          <p>
            A current 21-inch battery mower with a strong pack and a brushless motor cuts cleanly, starts at the press of a button, runs far more quietly and needs almost no routine maintenance. You have no oil to change, no carburetor to gum up over the winter and no fuel can sitting in the garage. For a lawn that fits inside the battery's real runtime, it is simply less work to own.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where does gas still come out ahead?</h3>

          <p>
            Gas keeps two advantages: runtime and steady power under heavy load. One tank mows a large yard without a break, and a gas engine does not fade as it grinds through thick, wet grass. Battery runtime is printed on the box for good conditions, and in long, damp spring growth the real figure can come up well short. If your lawn is larger than about half an acre, or you often let the grass grow long between mows, gas or a battery mower with two packs is the practical answer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which one costs less to own?</h3>

          <p>
            The running costs are closer than the price tags suggest. A gas mower needs oil, a spark plug, an air filter and fuel stabilizer every year, plus a carburetor cleaning now and then when fuel sits too long. A battery mower needs none of that, but the battery itself wears out: it gradually loses capacity, and a replacement high-capacity pack can cost a large share of what the mower did. If you already own batteries from the same tool platform, the case for battery gets stronger.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do noise and fumes matter?</h3>

          <p>
            For many owners they decide it. Battery mowers are quiet enough for an early weekend mow without bothering the neighbors, and there is no exhaust to breathe. If you mow close to a patio or under open windows, you will notice that difference every week. As long as the yard fits a battery mower's runtime, it is usually the nicer tool to own.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Quick rule</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Mostly flat and under a third of an acre? A battery mower is usually the smarter buy.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Past half an acre, on steep ground, or if tall, wet grass is a regular thing, pick a rear-drive gas mower or a battery mower with a second pack so you are not stopping halfway.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="yard-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Size Self-Propelled Mower Do You Need for Your Yard?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Size the mower to the grass, not the lot: a 21-inch self-propelled handles up to about a quarter acre of lawn in 30 to 45 minutes, a 30-inch walk-behind earns its place past half an acre, and beyond three quarters of an acre of open grass a riding mower usually makes more sense. The steps below turn that rule into a decision for your own yard.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you measure the grass you actually mow?</h3>

          <p>
            The size of the lot says less than the mowable area. A half-acre lot with a big house, a driveway, a patio and planting beds might hold only a quarter acre of real lawn. Pace it out, or use a satellite map tool to estimate the grass alone. Compare that number, not the figure on the deed, with battery runtime ratings and deck widths.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Up to a quarter acre of grass</h3>

          <p>
            Any type of 21-inch self-propelled mower handles this comfortably in 30 to 45 minutes. It is where battery models are at their best, and where a smaller, lighter mower is nicer to use than a heavy one. If the lawn is truly tiny, ask whether you need self-propulsion at all; our guide to the <a href="/best-lawn-mower-for-small-yard/">best mowers for small yards</a> covers cheaper push, reel and corded options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A quarter to half an acre</h3>

          <p>
            A strong 21- or 22-inch self-propelled is still the right tool here, but drive type and runtime begin to matter more. Gas mowers finish without a break, while battery mowers need a large pack or a spare. Plan on 45 minutes to an hour and a quarter, depending on how many obstacles you mow around.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Over half an acre</h3>

          <p>
            Walking starts to take a long time. A 30-inch walk-behind cuts the time noticeably and still fits through gates a rider cannot. Once you pass three quarters of an acre of open grass, though, a riding mower is usually the better tool. Our <a href="/best-riding-lawn-mower/">riding mower rankings</a> pick up where this guide stops, and for very large, flat properties the <a href="/best-zero-turn-mower/">zero-turn guide</a> is the next step.
          </p>

