        <div id="deck-and-cut" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Deck Width and Material Give the Best Cut?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 21-inch deck suits most yards, and deck material matters more for how long the mower lasts than for how well it cuts; sharp blades and a well-shaped deck decide the finish. Treat deck width as the last thing to upgrade, after you have chosen the drive layout and the power source.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is a wider deck worth it?</h3>

          <p>
            The 21-inch deck is the norm on self-propelled mowers because it balances coverage and maneuverability. Going to 22 inches adds a little ground on every pass. Going to 30 inches adds a lot, but it turns the mower into a much heavier machine that is harder to steer around beds and trees. Unless your lawn is large and open, the narrower deck is the easier mower to live with.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Steel, aluminum or polymer?</h3>

          <p>
            Stamped steel is the common choice and holds up well as long as you scrape off wet clippings and store it dry; neglected steel decks rust through at the seams after several seasons. Cast aluminum decks, found on premium mowers, do not rust and tend to outlive the engine. The polymer and composite decks on battery mowers also shrug off rust and keep the weight down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What actually makes a clean cut?</h3>

          <p>
            Blade sharpness, blade speed and deck shape do most of the work. A mulching deck keeps clippings circulating so the blade can chop them several times before they fall. Dual-blade and stacked-blade designs leave a cleaner finish and finer mulch, at the cost of slightly more load on the engine or battery. No deck rescues a dull blade, which tears the grass and leaves white, frayed tips that turn brown within a day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do you need a 3-in-1 deck?</h3>

          <p>
            If you want flexibility, yes: bag in spring and fall, mulch through the summer, and side-discharge when the grass gets ahead of you. Single-point or two-point height adjustment is also worth a small premium, because it makes raising the deck in hot weather quick, and cutting higher in summer is one of the simplest ways to keep a lawn healthy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="storage-weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Heavy Is a Self-Propelled Mower, and How Do You Store It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Expect a self-propelled mower to weigh more than a push mower, and check the listed weight and folded size before you buy if it has to climb steps or squeeze into a small garage. The drive adds a transmission or motor that you feel every time you lift the mower into a truck, carry it up steps or wrestle it around a tight shed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can a gas mower stand on its end?</h3>

          <p>
            Only if the engine is designed for it. Floor space is the hidden cost in small garages, and many newer mowers fold the handle flat while some stand upright. A gas mower that stores vertically needs an engine built for that position so oil does not run into the cylinder or the air filter. Battery mowers can stand up or hang on a wall with no such worry, which is one reason they suit townhomes and other small homes with a patch of lawn.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which handle features are worth having?</h3>

          <p>
            Handle design matters more than most buyers expect. A handle with more than one height setting suits both tall and short people in the same household, and a drive bail that is comfortable to hold for an hour saves sore hands. Owners commonly complain about drive levers on budget models that need a firm grip, so anyone with arthritis or weak grip should consider a pace-sensing handle worth the extra cost.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do wheel size and bearings matter?</h3>

          <p>
            On rough ground, yes. Larger rear wheels roll over bumps and holes more easily and are less likely to scalp the turf. Ball-bearing wheels outlast plain bushings on plastic hubs. Marketing rarely mentions either detail, yet these are the parts that tend to wear out first on cheaper mowers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Why Is My Self-Propelled Mower Not Pulling, and How Do You Fix It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most often the drive cable has stretched, the belt has glazed or grass has packed around the wheel gears, and all three are cheap home fixes. The drive is the part of a self-propelled mower that fails most, and nearly always through slow wear rather than sudden breakage. On gas mowers a belt runs from the engine to a transmission, and a cable from the handle tightens that belt when you squeeze the bail. Over time the cable stretches and the belt glazes, and the mower starts to feel weak or to slip under load.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Safety first</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Disable the mower before you touch the blade, belt or wheels.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Pull the spark plug wire on a gas mower, or remove the battery from a battery mower, and wait for the blade to stop completely. Wear heavy gloves when handling the blade, and follow the owner's manual on which way to tip a gas mower so oil and fuel stay where they belong.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Start with the drive cable</h3>

          <p>
            The cable adjuster is nearly always the first fix. Most mowers have a barrel or nut near the handle or at the transmission that takes up slack, and a few turns bring back full engagement. Check it every spring, before the first mow, rather than waiting for the drive to fade on a hill.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the belt next</h3>

          <p>
            If adjusting the cable does not help, take off the belt cover and look for a belt that is glazed, cracked or loose. Belts are inexpensive and count as a normal wear part every few seasons on a mower that is used regularly, so replacing one is routine maintenance, not a sign the mower is finished.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clear packed grass from the wheels</h3>

          <p>
            Clippings are the other usual suspect. They build up around the drive wheels, the pinion gears and the transmission pulley, where they act like a brake. Once a season, take the wheels off, clean out the gear teeth and make sure the small pawls inside each wheel move freely. Worn plastic wheel gears are a common, low-cost replacement part.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What do battery mowers need?</h3>

          <p>
            Battery mowers skip the belt and cable, but their wheels and drive gears still need to stay clean, and the battery should spend the winter indoors at a moderate charge. With either fuel, sharpen or replace the blade at least once a season, and if the drive quits entirely, rule out the simple causes before paying for a transmission. Our companion guide to <a href="/best-gas-lawn-mowers/">gas lawn mowers</a> walks through the full seasonal engine routine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="clippings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Should You Bag, Mulch or Side-Discharge With a Self-Propelled Mower?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mulch on a regularly mown, healthy lawn, bag when you want a clean finish, and side-discharge when the grass has run away from you. Each choice also changes how the drive behaves, which is worth knowing before you settle on front or rear drive.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How does bagging affect the drive?</h3>

          <p>
            A rear bag adds weight behind the rear axle as it fills, often several pounds of wet grass by the end of a row. That load helps a rear-drive mower grip and hurts a front-drive one, which is why owners who bag on any kind of slope are pointed toward rear-wheel drive.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When does mulching work best?</h3>

          <p>
            Mulching is the easiest option for healthy turf that gets mown on schedule. Clippings fall back as fine pieces that break down within days and return nitrogen to the soil. It only works well when you remove no more than about a third of the blade height at once. Let the grass grow long and mulching leaves wet clumps that smother the lawn and clog the deck.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When should you side-discharge?</h3>

          <p>
            Side discharge is the way out when the lawn has gotten away from you after a wet week or a trip. Fit the side chute, raise the deck and make a first pass to bring the height down. A few days later, make a second, lower pass with the mulch plug in to tidy up. Trying to bag or mulch overgrown grass in a single pass is how drive belts get overworked.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What makes a grass bag easy to live with?</h3>

          <p>
            Capacity and how easily the bag empties matter more than spec sheets let on. A bag with a rigid frame and a top handle is easier to lift off and dump than a floppy cloth sack. If you bag every week, make sure it clips on without a fight with the rear door, because you will repeat that move dozens of times a season.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. How Much Should You Spend on a Self-Propelled Mower?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most buyers should plan on roughly $450 to $700, the range where rear drive, better speed control and longer warranties appear. Prices quoted here are approximate at the time of writing; the tiers below show what each step up buys.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does $350 to $450 get you?</h3>

          <p>
            Entry gas self-propelled mowers from mainstream brands usually sell for roughly $350 to $450. That buys a 21-inch steel deck, front-wheel drive, often a single speed and a basic engine. For a flat lot and an owner who keeps up with oil and fuel stabilizer, it is all the mower needed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why is $450 to $700 the sweet spot?</h3>

          <p>
            This middle band is where most buyers belong. It brings rear-wheel drive, variable or pace-sensing speed, better handles and decks, and longer warranties. Battery kits at this level typically include one battery and a charger, and the better ones can mow a quarter acre or more on a charge.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When is $700 to $1,500 justified?</h3>

          <p>
            Premium walk-behinds in this range add cast aluminum decks, all-wheel drive, wider decks or bigger battery packs. They make sense for a large or steep property, for an owner who plans to keep the mower 10 years or more, or for anyone who wants a battery mower with enough capacity to stop thinking about runtime. If a 30-inch walk-behind you are pricing comes in above $1,200, compare it with an entry riding mower before you buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What extras should you budget for?</h3>

          <p>
            A second battery, a bagger that some models sell separately, a spare blade and a season of oil and filters all add up. Paying a little more to buy from a store with a local service counter or from an authorized dealer is usually worth it, because warranty work on a mower almost always means taking it somewhere.
          </p>

