        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes With Self-Propelled Mowers
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing front-wheel drive for a hilly lawn</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                As you push uphill, weight slides off the front wheels, and a filling grass bag makes the slipping worse. If any stretch of your lawn climbs noticeably, buy rear-wheel or all-wheel drive. Owners who skip this end up shoving a self-propelled mower up the same bank every week, which defeats the point of paying for the drive.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Believing the runtime printed on the battery box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Box runtimes describe ideal conditions, and long, damp or dense grass can shorten real runtime a lot. Measure the grass you actually mow, compare it honestly with the rating, and add a second battery if you are close to the limit. Going flat halfway across the lawn is the complaint battery-mower owners raise most often.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Writing off the drive before adjusting the cable</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A drive that feels weak usually has a stretched cable, not a failed transmission. Most mowers have a simple adjuster near the handle or the transmission that restores full engagement within a minute. Check it every spring before you price a replacement transmission or decide the whole mower has simply worn out.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Racing through tall grass at full speed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Driving a self-propelled mower fast through overgrown grass leaves a ragged cut, clogs the chute and bag, and drains batteries. Raise the deck, slow the drive and take narrower passes. The job ends sooner overall because you are not stopping to clear clogs, and the smaller bites keep the drive from straining.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for a drive a small lawn does not need</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On a small, flat lawn of a few thousand square feet, a self-propel drive only adds weight, cost and parts that can break. A light push mower or a compact battery mower handles the job well and is easier to store and carry. Put the money you save toward a sharp spare blade instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

