        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which self-propelled mower is best for most households?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Toro Recycler 22-inch Personal Pace is the safest all-round choice for a flat-to-rolling suburban yard, because its speed-sensing drive needs no fiddling and its deck mulches well. If you would rather leave gas behind, the EGO LM2135SP is the stronger battery option, particularly on slopes, where its rear-wheel drive and 56-volt battery system hold their grip.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is rear-wheel drive better than front-wheel drive on a mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On slopes and when bagging, yes. Climbing a hill and filling the bag both shift weight onto the rear wheels, so rear drive gains grip just as front drive loses it. Front-wheel drive turns more easily, because you can lift the drive wheels and pivot. Flat lots full of obstacles suit front drive; any noticeable slope calls for rear drive.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a battery self-propelled mower handle half an acre?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some can, though usually only with a large battery or a second pack. Runtimes on the box assume ideal conditions, and dense or damp grass cuts them short. For half an acre of actual lawn, buy a kit with two batteries or a model with dual battery ports. Under a quarter acre, one good battery normally finishes with room to spare.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a self-propelled lawn mower last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With regular maintenance, a gas self-propelled mower from a mainstream brand commonly lasts eight to ten years, and premium models with aluminum decks often run longer. Expect to replace the drive belt and cable every few seasons as normal wear. Battery mowers have fewer wear parts, but the battery slowly loses capacity and may need replacing after several years.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my self-propelled mower struggle going uphill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually the cause is a stretched drive cable, a worn or glazed belt, grass packed around the wheel gears, or a front-wheel-drive layout losing grip as weight moves backward. Adjust the cable first, then clean out the wheels and inspect the belt. If the mower is front-drive and the bank is steep, the drive layout itself may be the limit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a self-propelled mower worth it for a small lawn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often not. On a small, flat lawn, a light push mower or a compact battery mower costs less, stores more easily and has fewer parts to fail. Self-propulsion pays its way when the yard has slopes, when you bag clippings regularly, or when each mow lasts long enough that pushing starts to wear you out.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 30-inch walk-behind mower better than a riding mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                That depends on access and terrain. A 30-inch walk-behind fits through standard gates, copes with terraces and steep banks that are unsafe for riders, and needs less storage space. A riding mower is quicker and less tiring on large, open, mostly level lawns. Once a property passes three quarters of an acre of open grass, a rider is usually the better value.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

